CH = dict(num=12, title="Reproduction: How Life Continues", tag="Biology", theme="Asexual and sexual reproduction in plants, animals and humans",
 intro="Reproduction is the biological process by which living beings produce new individuals of their own kind, so life continues even though individuals die. This chapter compares asexual and sexual reproduction, studies vegetative propagation, flowers, pollination and fertilisation, animal strategies, and the human reproductive system, menstrual cycle, pregnancy and reproductive health.",
 big_questions=["When does a farmer prefer asexual or sexual methods of crop production?", "Why do complex animals and flowering plants use sexual reproduction while simple organisms reproduce mainly asexually?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "12.1 Asexual Reproduction"),
 ("p", "In **asexual reproduction** a single parent produces offspring that are almost exact copies. In **sexual reproduction** two parents contribute, and offspring inherit a mix of characters, giving variation that helps adaptation and may lead to new species."),
 ("table", ["Feature", "Asexual", "Sexual"], [
   ["Parents", "One", "Two (male and female gametes)"], ["Offspring", "Genetically identical (**clones**)", "Show variation"],
   ["Basis", "**Mitosis**", "**Meiosis** (gametes) + fertilisation"], ["Speed", "Fast; quick population rise in favourable conditions", "Slower"],
   ["Examples", "Bacteria, amoeba, yeast, hydra, sponge, potato, *Bryophyllum*", "Flowering plants, most animals, humans"]]),
 ("h3", "Vegetative propagation in plants"),
 ("p", "New plants arise from vegetative parts (growing parts): potato and ginger (underground stems), money plant and sugarcane (stem cuttings), *Bryophyllum* (plantlets on leaf edges)."),
 ("table", ["Method", "How it is done", "Notes"], [
   ["**Cutting**", "Cut shoot with nodes and internodes (collected in the morning); remove lower leaves; insert half the length in compost-mixed soil at 45–60°; water regularly", "Rose, sugarcane, money plant"],
   ["**Grafting**", "Slit a rooted plant (stock, Plant A); fit a stem piece (scion, Plant B) into it; wrap with cloth/film to keep pests out until it heals", "Combines good traits, e.g. fruit trees, roses; KVKs of ICAR train farmers"],
   ["**Layering**", "Bury the middle of a flexible twig (e.g. lemon) in soil; roots form in 10–15 days; then cut from parent", "Plant remains attached to the parent while rooting"],
   ["**Tissue culture**", "Grow plantlets from shoot tip (apical meristem) in nutrient medium", "Mass-produces healthy, virus-free banana plantlets; high yield"]]),
 ("h3", "Other asexual methods"),
 ("ul", ["**Budding**: in yeast (activity: yeast in sugar solution under a microscope shows small round buds) and in *Hydra* (repeated cell division at one site forms a bud that grows and detaches; many buds may appear together).", "**Spore formation**: moulds like *Rhizopus* and *Aspergillus* produce millions of light, usually single-celled spores in sacs or on vesicles at hyphal tips; spores float in air and germinate in moisture and nutrients (so bread grows mould in a warm moist chamber, 25–35 °C; refrigeration slows this).", "**Louis Pasteur** proved that life comes from pre-existing life, ending spontaneous generation, and supported germ theory and sterilisation.", "Fungi are useful: degrade wastes and pollutants, remove heavy metals, give antibiotics (penicillin)."]),
 ("note", "IMPORTANT", "In all asexual methods the central process is **mitosis**, so the offspring are **genetically identical clones** of the parent. Uniform clones are however more vulnerable to the same disease."),
 ("h2", "12.2 Sexual Reproduction"),
 ("h3", "Meiosis and variation"),
 ("ul", ["Each species has a fixed chromosome number. If both parents gave full sets, numbers would double each generation; **meiosis** solves this.", "Humans have 23 pairs (46) chromosomes. In meiosis a **diploid** cell becomes **haploid** gametes (23 chromosomes): sperm and egg in animals; pollen (male gametes) and ovule (egg) in plants.", "Each gamete gets one chromosome from every pair. Bead activity: with just 3 pairs of characters, 2<sup>3</sup> = **8 combinations** are possible; with 23 pairs the number is huge, so children differ from parents and siblings.", "Variation helps some individuals survive changes (e.g. tolerance of low oxygen at altitude, adult milk digestion) and drives evolution."]),
 ("h3", "Flower — the reproductive organ of angiosperms"),
 ("table", ["Whorl / part", "Sub-parts", "Function"], [
   ["**Sepals**", "Green outermost whorl", "Protect the bud and the flower"],
   ["**Petals**", "Coloured, often fragrant", "Attract pollinators"],
   ["**Stamen** (male)", "Filament + **anther** (makes pollen grains with male gametes)", "Produces male gametes"],
   ["**Pistil** (female)", "**Stigma** (tip, sticky/flat), **style** (tube), **ovary** (contains ovules; each ovule has an egg cell)", "Receives pollen; produces egg"]]),
 ("p", "A complete flower has all four whorls. Pea-plant activity: wrapping or removing stamens shows that fruit forms only when pollen reaches the stigma; fruit formed in all treatments except the bud whose stamens were removed and bagged."),
 ("h3", "Pollination"),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "**Pollination**: transfer of pollen from anther to stigma. **Self-pollination**: to the stigma of the same flower or another flower of the same plant. **Cross-pollination**: to a flower of another plant of the same kind (increases variation)."),
 ("table", ["Agent", "Plant examples", "Adaptations"], [
   ["Wind", "Wheat, maize, rice", "Light small pollen in huge numbers; long feathery stigma"],
   ["Water", "*Vallisneria*, *Hydrilla*", "Water currents carry pollen"],
   ["Insects", "Sunflower, hibiscus, marigold", "Bright colour, nectar, fragrance; large sticky or spiny pollen; sticky stigma"],
   ["Birds", "Coral tree, hibiscus", "Visited by sunbirds, white-eyes"]]),
 ("table", ["Strategy", "Pollen per flower", "Seeds formed"], [["Wind (maize, wheat)", "5,00,000 – 10,00,000", "50 – 200"], ["Insect (sunflower)", "20,000 – 40,000", "800 – 1,000"]]),
 ("p", "Insect pollination wastes less pollen (higher seeds per pollen); wind pollination compensates with very large numbers of pollen grains."),
 ("h3", "Fertilisation and seed formation"),
 ("steps", [("Pollen lands", "On a compatible stigma, a pollen grain germinates into a **pollen tube**."), ("Tube grows", "Down the style into the ovary; the male gamete travels through it to the ovule."), ("Fusion", "Male gamete fuses with the egg: **fertilisation**; the fertilised egg is a **zygote**, which becomes an embryo."), ("Fruit and seed", "**Ovule → seed; ovary → fruit.** Seeds are dispersed by wind, water or animals and germinate in favourable conditions.")]),
 ("ul", ["Plant breeding uses sexual reproduction: **selective breeding**, **artificial hybridisation** (remove stamens, bag the flower, hand-pollinate with chosen pollen) and **genetic engineering** to get high-yield and disease-resistant varieties.", "P. Maheshwari, 'Father of Indian Embryology', developed in-vitro fertilisation in flowering plants."]),
 ("h2", "12.3 Reproduction in Animals"),
 ("table", ["Type", "Where fertilisation occurs", "Examples", "Consequence"], [
   ["**External fertilisation**", "Outside the body, in water; female releases eggs, male releases sperm over them", "Fish, frogs", "Many eggs (fish hundreds–thousands; frog 5,000–50,000) as many are lost; survival low"],
   ["**Internal fertilisation**", "Inside the female body", "Reptiles (lizard 2–20 eggs), birds (1–15 eggs), mammals", "Fewer eggs; better protection; survival moderate to high"]]),
 ("ul", ["Fish, amphibians and insects lay many yolk-poor eggs; the **larva** hatches, feeds, and then transforms (e.g. butterfly: egg → larva → pupa → adult; frog: tadpole).", "Reptiles and birds lay eggs with enough yolk to nourish the embryo until hatching; in mammals the zygote develops inside the mother and the young are fed milk.", "Care after birth depends on how developed the young are at hatching or birth."]),
 ("h2", "12.4 Reproduction in Human Beings"),
 ("p", "At reproductive maturity (puberty) organs mature and produce gametes. A sperm fusing with an egg gives a zygote → embryo → **foetus** (from about the ninth week) in the **uterus**."),
 ("table", ["System", "Organs and functions"], [
   ["Male", "**Testes** (in the **scrotum**, which keeps them slightly cooler) make sperm and a hormone; **vas deferens** carries sperm to the **urethra** (common passage for urine and sperm); **seminal vesicles and prostate** add nourishing fluid; the sperm has a head (genetic material) and a tail for swimming"],
   ["Female", "**Ovaries** make eggs and hormones; **oviducts (fallopian tubes)** carry the egg to the **uterus** (bag-like, where the foetus develops); it opens into the **vagina** through the **cervix**"]]),
 ("table", ["Feature", "Sperm", "Egg"], [["Size", "Very small", "Large"], ["Number", "Millions", "Few"], ["Stored nutrients", "Absent", "Present"], ["Motility", "Actively motile", "Non-motile"]]),
 ("p", "**Gametogenesis** (formation of gametes in testes and ovaries) is by meiosis: gametes have 23 chromosomes; the zygote regains 46. Males form numerous motile sperm, females usually one large egg at a time."),
 ("h3", "Fertilisation, implantation and the menstrual cycle"),
 ("ul", ["A girl's ovaries hold millions of immature eggs from birth. From puberty usually **one mature egg is released each month** (**ovulation**) and the uterine lining thickens with blood vessels.", "If a sperm fuses with the egg in the oviduct a **zygote** forms; it divides mitotically while moving to the uterus and **implants** in the lining: the start of **pregnancy** (menstrual cycle stops).", "If the egg is not fertilised it lives about a day, then the lining breaks down and leaves with blood through the vagina: **menstruation** (3–7 days).", "The cycle repeats every 21–35 days (often 28), starting at puberty (10–14 years) and continuing until menopause (about 50 years)."]),
 ("table", ["Days (28-day cycle)", "Event"], [["1–5", "Menstruation: lining sheds"], ["6–14", "Lining rebuilds; egg matures in ovary"], ["About 14", "Ovulation: ovary releases a mature egg"], ["15–28", "Lining thickens; if no fertilisation, it starts breaking down around day 28"]]),
 ("note", "REMEMBER", "**Sex determination**: females have XX, males XY. The mother always gives X; the **father's gamete (X or Y)** decides the baby's sex."),
 ("h3", "Pregnancy, childbirth and care"),
 ("ul", ["Pregnancy lasts about **nine months** in three **trimesters**: 1st — embryo forms, major organs start; 2nd — foetus grows, movements felt; 3rd — rapid growth for life outside. Strong uterine contractions push the baby out; doctors may help when a normal birth is unsafe.", "Newborn care: **breastfeeding** (complete nutrition, protects from disease), warmth, vaccination, gentle handling.", "Mother needs a balanced diet, check-ups, rest, emotional support, and should avoid smoking, alcohol and medicines not advised by a doctor. Post-partum depression is treatable; **ASHA workers** (over 10 lakh) support maternal care, safe deliveries and immunisation.", "**IVF**: egg and sperm combined in a laboratory dish and the embryo placed in the uterus ('test tube baby'); India's first, Kanupriya Agarwal (Durga), was born in 1978 through work of Subhash Mukhopadhyay.", "**Menstrual hygiene**: use pads/tampons/cups, change pads every 4–6 hours, wash with water, wash hands with soap before and after, dispose pads wrapped (not flushed), dry reusable pads fully. Menstruation is a sign of a healthy system."]),
 ("h3", "Maturity, STIs and birth control"),
 ("ul", ["Sexual maturity (sperm production, menstrual cycles) is gradual, but **emotional maturity** takes longer; responsible choices prevent unplanned pregnancy and infection.", "**STIs**: gonorrhoea, herpes, syphilis, genital warts, **HIV** (can lead to AIDS); some are incurable; condoms reduce transmission and prevent pregnancy.", "Birth control: **barriers** (condoms, vaginal covers); **oral pills** (hormonal; may have side effects, no protection against STIs); **IUDs** such as **Copper-T**; **surgical** blocking of vas deferens or fallopian tubes.", "Abortion (generally within the first trimester) is medical; **prenatal sex determination is illegal in India** to protect the sex ratio.", "CDRI Lucknow developed a once-a-week non-steroidal, non-hormonal oral contraceptive."]),
 ("h2", "12.5 Quick Exam Questions"),
 ("qa", [
   ("Why does asexual reproduction give identical offspring?", "It involves only one parent and mitosis, which copies the chromosomes exactly."),
   ("What is vegetative propagation? Give examples and advantages.", "New plants from vegetative parts (potato, ginger, Bryophyllum); gives identical plants quickly and on a large scale."),
   ("Differentiate cutting, grafting and layering.", "Cutting: a piece planted; grafting: scion fitted on a rooted stock; layering: buried twig roots while attached to parent."),
   ("How do yeast and hydra reproduce?", "By budding: an outgrowth forms, enlarges and separates."),
   ("Why does bread get mould?", "Airborne spores settle on moist, warm bread and germinate rapidly; refrigeration slows this."),
   ("Why is meiosis necessary in sexual reproduction?", "It halves the chromosome number so the zygote regains the normal number and creates variation."),
   ("Name the parts of a flower and their functions.", "Sepals protect, petals attract, stamen produces pollen, pistil (stigma, style, ovary) receives pollen and holds ovules."),
   ("Differentiate self- and cross-pollination.", "Self: same flower or plant; cross: between flowers of different plants of the same kind."),
   ("How is wind pollination adapted?", "Light pollen in huge numbers and long feathery stigma."),
   ("Describe fertilisation in flowering plants.", "Pollen tube grows to the ovule, male gamete fuses with the egg forming a zygote; ovule becomes seed, ovary fruit."),
   ("Differentiate external and internal fertilisation.", "External: outside body in water, many eggs, low survival; internal: inside the female, fewer eggs, better survival."),
   ("What is the function of testes, ovary, uterus?", "Testes make sperm; ovaries make eggs; the uterus nourishes the developing foetus."),
   ("What is menstruation and why does it occur?", "Shedding of the uterine lining with blood when the egg is not fertilised."),
   ("Why does the menstrual cycle stop in pregnancy?", "The implanted embryo needs the thick lining, so it is not shed."),
   ("Who determines the sex of the baby and how?", "The father: X sperm gives a girl (XX), Y sperm a boy (XY)."),
   ("Name two methods of birth control and one protection against STIs.", "IUD (Copper-T) and oral pills; condoms protect against STIs."),
 ])],
 takeaways=["**Asexual**: one parent, mitosis, clones (budding, spores, vegetative propagation, tissue culture); **sexual**: two parents, meiosis, variation.", "Flower: **sepal, petal, stamen (anther), pistil (stigma, style, ovary)**; **pollination → fertilisation → zygote**; **ovule → seed, ovary → fruit**.", "External fertilisation (fish, frog) needs many eggs; internal (reptiles, birds, mammals) gives better survival.", "Human gametes have **23** chromosomes; zygote **46**; **XX/XY** — the father decides sex.", "Menstrual cycle ≈ **28 days**; **ovulation** about day 14; menstruation lasts 3–7 days; pregnancy ≈ **9 months** (3 trimesters).", "Promote hygiene, balanced diet, and use **condoms/contraceptives** responsibly; sex determination before birth is illegal."])
